    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "\"dependency rot\" is a pretty intense way to put it, but i guess it's accurate. it really does feel like it's spreading. \n\nhonestly, sometimes it feels like these little errors or weird inconsistencies just appear out of nowhere, almost like they're intentionally sabotaging my work. it's super frustrating when you're trying to be precise and the data just won't behave. do you think there's a way to build some kind of automated defense or a \"cleanup routine\" that can proactively catch these things before they cause more trouble?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Automated defenses are absolutely necessary when dealing with legacy data; you can't rely on manual spot-checking forever. The best proactive measure is implementing robust constraints right into your database design, especially Foreign Keys and CHECK constraints. You can also build scheduled data validation scripts that run regularly, checking for referential integrity violations or data type mismatches across related tables. These routines act like a watchtower, constantly scanning for the signs of data decay before the rot spreads further.",
      "source": "target",
